description:
S18 Layer 1 View feedback fixes (2026-07-25). The PRODUCTION CODE FOR THIS ITEM IS ALREADY WRITTEN, both tsconfigs typecheck clean, and every pre-existing test passes — what is missing is the test coverage described below. Plan: /Users/matkatmusicllc/.claude/plans/feedback-on-the-app-layer1-html-sorted-shore.md. Implementation notes (read these first; they record two deviations from the plan): plans/implementation-notes-feedback-on-the-app-layer1-html-sorted-shore.md. Work in RevEng/jfred, never in a ~/Programming/jfred clone. Runner is `node --test` via `npm test` (NOT vitest); a single file runs as `npx tsx --test tests/<file>`. Follow plans/coding-requirements.md: 4-space indent, verb-named functions, domain types over primitives, no re-export shims, tests named test_<behavior> carrying plain-English Scenario/Steps comments.

Run these IN ORDER, stopping at the first failure.

1. TYPECHECK: `npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json` and `npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.webapp.json`. Both were clean at handoff.

2. FULL SUITE: `npm test`. This has NOT been run end to end since these changes — that is the single biggest open risk in this item. Changed/new files: tests/layer1_repo_tree.test.ts, tests/layer1_disk_walk.test.ts, tests/layer1_ruler_axis.test.ts, tests/layer1-view-test-helpers.ts, tests/viewer_api_layer1_placement.test.ts (comments only), tests/layer1-acceptance.test.ts, tests/layer1-page.test.ts, tests/viewer-console-links.test.ts (import path only), tests/webapp-dom-test-helpers.ts, plus NEW tests/layer1_view_progress.test.ts and NEW tests/viewer_api_layer1_route.test.ts. Each of those was verified individually; only the whole-suite interaction is unproven.
   TRAP A: tests/layer1-acceptance.test.ts gained a submodule case AND now drives a STREAMED response through forwardFetchToOrigin. Node's undici does support response.body.getReader(), so it should pass; if happy-dom intercepts and returns a bodiless Response, the documented fallback is to have the acceptance test stub /api/layer1-view WITHOUT progress=1 (the plain path is fully supported and tests/viewer_api_layer1_route.test.ts already proves the two paths return the same view).
   TRAP B: known port-collision flake. These files pick a port as `BASE + process.pid % 500` (17400/17900/18400/18900/19400/19900/20400 are all claimed). A leftover viewer from a prior run answers 400 to EVERY request, which looks nothing like a port problem — it presents as the endpoint rejecting valid input. Re-run before debugging.

3. WEBAPP BUILD: `npm run build:webapp`. The new modules webapp/app-ndjson.ts, webapp/layer1-progress.ts and webapp/layer1-zoom.ts must land in webapp/dist/. tsconfig.webapp.json includes the whole webapp directory by glob, so nothing needs registering; it emits per-file rather than bundling, and the browser resolves ./layer1-progress.js at runtime.

4. LIVE SMOKE — this is the run the feedback came from. `npm run app`, then open http://127.0.0.1:7343/app/layer1.html?dir=<abs path to RevEng/jfred>&repo=<same path> and confirm, in order:
   - the progress strip appears IMMEDIATELY and its label counts `reading file history \u2014 N / 833` while the bar fills, then disappears when the timeline draws;
   - the crumb reads 833 pairs / 0 repo-only / 1 disk-only. Measured in-process at handoff: before the fix it was 832 / 4 / 6415. The single remaining disk-only row is a genuinely untracked file. NO bucket row may name scenarios, jfredToolsPlugin, external/tmux_lib or external/claude_plugin_lib;
   - no two commit dots overlap and no two node labels overprint (the 16 px floor);
   - every node label is 8 characters, and hovering one shows the full 40;
   - the \u2212 / + / Reset buttons change the render's size and the readout tracks;
   - SCROLL ALL THE WAY RIGHT: the ruler stays pinned at the left edge and its ticks still line up vertically with the nodes beside them. Then repeat that scroll at 50% and at 200% zoom.
   That last bullet is the plan's RISK #1 and the one thing in this whole item that could not be verified from source: `position: sticky` inside an element carrying the native CSS `zoom` property (a zoomed element establishes its own zoom context, and the sticky gutter is its descendant). If sticky misbehaves, the documented fallback is to move `zoom: var(--zoom, 1)` from `.canvas` up to `main.timelines`, which puts the sticky child inside a zoomed SCROLL CONTAINER — the ordinary case. `.canvas` was tried first because it keeps the scroll container's own padding unscaled.

5. SPEC UPDATE: specs/from-scratch-SPEC.md, the S18 section (starts around line 262). Record: the 16 px per-gap FLOOR and the cap raised 24 \u2192 120, which SUPERSEDES the 24 px cap locked earlier in that same section's "Ruler scale \u2014 capped linear" paragraph (the axis is now floored-linear-capped); submodule exclusion, achieved by asking git rather than by hand — walkCurrentFileState now runs `git ls-files -z --cached --others --exclude-standard` in the project folder, which stops at a gitlink exactly as `git ls-tree -r` does and additionally honors nested .gitignore files, .git/info/exclude and core.excludesFile, with the hand-rolled walk retained ONLY as the fallback for a project folder that is in no git repository (this also means the "Pairing" paragraph's disk-walk sentence needs rewording), while readRepoTreeAtRef (renamed from listRepoTreeAtRef) splits the tree into trackedFiles and submodulePaths so a gitlink is never paired as a file; the ?progress=1 NDJSON stream and the src/viewer_api_layer1.ts \u2192 src/viewer_api_layer1_route.ts route split it forced (the builder now knows nothing about HTTP, and importing the other way would be a cycle since the stream must call the builder WITH its progress sink); the sticky ruler gutter; the zoom control; and the 8-character hash labels. The section's closing "Known cosmetic issue, deliberately not solved" bullet about overlapping dots is now SOLVED by the floor and must be updated rather than left standing.
